  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/cyphotilapia-frontosa-2/",
      "html": "<p>The humpheaded Frontosa (Cyphotilapia frontosa) from Lake Tanganyika has enjoyed great popularity for decades, although (or because) the species is one of the bulls among the cichlids. Old males, which look bizarre with their forehead hump, can grow to 30 cm. Females generally remain smaller.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "beforeYouBuy",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/cyphotilapia-frontosa-2/",
      "html": "<p>C. frontosa has always been one of the more expensive fish. Wild catches have to be collected by diving with compressed air tanks, because they live at a depth of 30-40 metres. They then have to be decompressed slowly over several days, which is costly.</p><p>There are several geographic colour varieties of the species distributed throughout Lake Tanganyika. One has six body stripes plus a stripe through the eye, for a total of seven stripes; most have five body stripes plus the eye stripe, for a total of six. When looking for a specific variant, be careful: trade designations sometimes refer only to the body stripes and sometimes to the total number of stripes.</p><p>Captive-bred six-striped specimens from Taiwan stand out due to the beautiful blue coloration of the fins. The dark body stripes contrast nicely with the light, also bluish background. Animals of 4-6 cm show no sign of a head bump yet, and sex determination is still impossible at this size.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/cyphotilapia-frontosa-2/",
      "html": "<p>C. frontosa is a crepuscular predator that prefers to feed on small fish and shrimp.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/cyphotilapia-frontosa-2/",
      "html": "<p>Old males develop a forehead hump and can reach 30 cm; females generally remain smaller.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/cyphotilapia-frontosa-2/",
      "html": "<p>Frontosa are mouthbrooders. Breeding succeeds relatively easily, but the fish are not very productive and the young grow very slowly.</p>"
    }
  ],
